export const DE_C2_S07_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-der-kaffeeriecher": [
    `In the year 1781, when His Majesty the King of Prussia decided that his subjects were spending too much money on foreign beans and too little on home-brewed beer, on which, after all, he himself had been raised, the roasting of coffee became a matter of state. From then on only the royal monopoly was allowed to roast, in sealed drums and at prices that made even a wealthy cloth merchant swallow twice before he placed an order. And because a ban without inspectors is merely a recommendation, inspectors were hired: discharged soldiers who had to be provided for anyway, men with good noses and little to do. The people called them the coffee sniffers.`,
    `Gottlieb Krause was one of them. At Torgau an Austrian canister shot had taken his left hand and, as he liked to say, sharpened his sense of smell in return, as if the Lord had thought some compensation appropriate. In the mornings he walked through the lanes of Brandenburg's New Town, his nose slightly raised like a hunting dog that isn't quite sure whether it ought to be ashamed of its work. He smelled tallow and peat, horse dung and sourdough, the tannery on the Havel and the widow Pagel's eternal cabbage dish. And he smelled, not at all rarely, that dark, forbidden, unmistakable aroma that crept out of cellar hatches and back yards.`,
    `Mostly he did his duty. He knocked, he showed his warrant, he confiscated the pan and wrote out the fine, and people cursed him behind their hands, which was a matter of indifference to him, because nobody had greeted him before either. A quarter of the fines was his due. That, the excise inspector had explained to him, was "the reward of virtue, in hard cash".`,
    `But in the house of the widow Dorothea Liebig on the Molkenmarkt, one November morning, the smell was so strong that he stopped as if he had walked into a wall. She opened the door herself, a woman of about forty, her hair not quite tamed under her bonnet, a streak of soot on her cheek. "I know who you are," she said, before he had taken his warrant out of his pocket. "And you know what I'm doing. Shall we spare ourselves the formalities? It's barley coffee. The king has allowed that, after all. Drink a cup, then you can bear witness to it."`,
    `It was not barley coffee. He smelled that, and he tasted it, and it tasted to him the way coffee had always tasted to him, namely revolting: as bitter as a lost lawsuit. He drank the cup to the bottom all the same, sitting in her kitchen with his one hand round the mug, and listened as she told him about her husband, a coppersmith who had beaten the roasting pan out himself a year before the fever took him. "It's the only thing I have left of him that still works," she said. Krause stood up, thanked her and wrote in his official book: "Liebig, widow. Barley. Nothing found."`,
    `He came back. At first every fortnight, for official reasons, as he told himself, then every Wednesday, for reasons he no longer told himself anything about. He learned that she got her beans from a bargeman who brought them up the Havel in flour sacks, that she read Gellert and the Bible only at funerals, and that she spoke about his missing hand only once, namely to say he might lend her the other one, because of the grinder, which was stuck. His book filled up with barley. Once the excise inspector remarked with a frown that nowhere in the whole Electoral March was as much barley drunk as on the Molkenmarkt, and Krause answered with the most guileless face at his disposal that the widow was simply a patriot.`,
    `In August 1786 the king died at Sanssouci, and by the following spring his nephew had abolished the hated monopoly. The coffee sniffers were dismissed with a word of thanks that weighed less than their last pay. The night after, Gottlieb Krause lay awake for a long time and understood that he now had no reason left to knock on doors on the Molkenmarkt. That, not the pay, was the real misfortune.`,
    `He knocked anyway. She opened, her bonnet neat this time, no streak of soot. "Herr Krause," she said, and it was the first time she hadn't addressed him in the old condescending third person. "You know roasting is allowed now. There's nothing left to sniff here." He took off his hat, turned it in his one hand and said he had something to confess to her: he didn't like coffee at all. He had never liked it. It was too bitter for him.`,
    `She looked at him for a while. Then she stepped aside and let him in. "I know," she said. "You pulled a face every time, like a child taking cod-liver oil. Why do you think I've poured you nothing but barley since the second winter?" Gottlieb Krause claimed later, when he sat by the stove in the evenings and his wife put the mug down in front of him, that his official book had been the only entirely truthful document the Prussian coffee administration had ever produced.`,
  ],
  "de-der-akt-pospischil": [
    `When the Imperial-Royal Ministry of Public Works was founded in the summer of 1908, it inherited from its predecessors, the way a nephew inherits from a rich aunt, mainly furniture and paper. The paper came across the Ring in handcarts, in tied-up bundles whose dust made the office porters cough, and a not inconsiderable part of it ended up on the desk of the ministerial junior clerk Dr Ferdinand Wondraschek, who was young, ambitious and consequently responsible for everything nobody else wanted to be responsible for.`,
    `At the very bottom of one of these bundles lay the Pospischil file. It was as thick as a hymnal and as yellow as an old set of teeth, and it began with a petition dated 14 March 1867, in which a certain Jan Pospischil, mayor of the Moravian municipality of Unter-Lhota, most humbly requested a bridge over the Bečva, since the ford was impassable at high water and the doctor, the priest and the midwife could sometimes only be greeted from the other bank. There followed forty-one years of processing.`,
    `Wondraschek read them over three afternoons with growing admiration. The file had wandered through four ministries, two governorates and a court commission. It had been forwarded "for your kind information", submitted "for comment", kept "on record" and once, in 1879, sent back "for lack of jurisdiction" to an office that had since been dissolved, whereupon it remained lost for six years, until an archivist found it in a cupboard that was actually meant for storing the flags for the Corpus Christi procession. Three generations of officials had left their initials, small intertwined marks that looked like the tracks of birds in the snow. Nobody had built a bridge. But nobody had refused one either. In Austria, Wondraschek thought, "no" is a sign of rudeness, "yes" a sign of recklessness, and in between lies the administration.`,
    `He made a decision that struck even him as audacious: he wanted to close the file. Not reject it, not approve it, but close it, lay it ad acta, the way one buries the dead, with dignity and a final note. For that, however, he needed a reason, and the only reason the regulations accepted was that the matter had been dealt with. So, without asking his head of section, which was later held against him as the only mistake of his career, he wrote to the district authority in Mährisch-Weißkirchen and asked for information on the current state of the Unter-Lhota bridge matter.`,
    `The answer came after three weeks, which in this authority was the equivalent of a telegram. Enclosed with it was a second letter in neat, somewhat old-fashioned German script, from a certain Václav Pospischil, mayor of Unter-Lhota, grandson of the petitioner. The bridge, he wrote, had been standing since the autumn of 1869. After two years of waiting, his grandfather had decided that the esteemed ministry surely had more important things to do, and the village had built the bridge from its own oaks and with its own hands. With all due reverence, they had named it the Minister's Bridge because, as his grandfather had said at the opening, "it too came into being without asking us".`,
    `Wondraschek laughed so loudly that the office porter put his head round the door. Then he read on. Now that the esteemed ministry was gratifyingly enquiring about the bridge again, the grandson wrote, the village most humbly took the liberty of pointing out that after thirty-nine years the oak beams were rotten, that the middle pier had swayed alarmingly in the last flood, and that they would be heartily grateful for a grant towards its repair. The letter ended with the sentence: "We have time, but the bridge has none left."`,
    `That evening Dr Ferdinand Wondraschek stayed at the office later than ever before. Beneath the last sheet of the old file, with his finest pen, he wrote: "Matter settled in 1869 by the self-help of the municipality. Noted with the expression of the highest esteem. Ad acta." He put his initials underneath, the first in this file to bring anything to an end, and tied up the bundle with a new string.`,
    `Then he took a fresh folder from the drawer, put the grandson's letter into it and wrote in the corner where the file number belonged: "Pospischil II". He looked at the label for a while, with a mixture of tenderness and horror that he later found hard to describe, and did a sum in his head. He was twenty-eight. If everything took its proper course, he would retire before this file did. He put it on top, right on top, and, for the first time in his working life, wrote on it the word "Urgent".`,
  ],
  "de-scardanelli": [
    `Merle Kaufmann was writing a dissertation on Hölderlin's late work that nobody but her supervisor would ever read, and to finance this certainty she spent her summers punting tourists up and down the Neckar. She was good at it. She had the pole under control and the town in her head, and when the boat glided past the yellow tower in which the poet had spent the second half of his life, deranged, as people said, cared for by the family of the carpenter Zimmer, she lowered her voice like a tour guide in a cathedral.`,
    `Then she told anecdotes. Some of them were true. Most she had made up, back in her first summer, because the truth was too thin for a fifty-minute boat trip and the Americans, the Swabians and the stag parties all wanted to hear something touching. Hölderlin, she claimed, had shaken hands with the river every morning. He had given the ducks the names of Greek gods and had been offended when they didn't answer to them. Once he had received a visitor only because the man wore a cherry leaf on his hat. Afterwards people photographed the tower with different eyes, and the tips were excellent.`,
    `In June she found the first slip of paper. It was stuck under the seat of her punt, which lay moored overnight by the Platanenallee: a folded sheet from a squared arithmetic exercise book. On it, in pencil, in very old and very careful handwriting, were eight lines about summer, rhymed, a little stiff, with a strangely floating serenity, as if someone were looking at a meadow from a great distance. Underneath it said: "With humility, Scardanelli. The 3rd of March 1748."`,
    `Merle knew the signature. With this invented name and such impossible dates Hölderlin had signed, in his last years, the little poems visitors asked him for, as if he himself no longer quite belonged in time. At first she suspected Jonas from the advanced seminar, whose humour was the kind that ends in a footnote. Jonas swore he had nothing to do with it, and with an indignation too genuine to be put on: his forgery, he said, would have rhymed better.`,
    `More slips came, roughly one a week. Poems about the Neckar, about the light in July, about a garden in which the beans wouldn't grow. Dated 1759, 1671, once 1940. Merle did what a literature scholar short of money does: she read them aloud. When the punt glided beneath the windows of the Bursagasse, she pulled the sheet out of her pocket and recited it as if it had only just surfaced from an archive, and the tourists held their breath, and she kept quiet about the archive being a boat seat.`,
    `At the end of July she noticed the woman. Every afternoon she sat at the open window of a narrow house below the tower, very old, very white, a cushion at her back, and she didn't stir at the anecdotes, nor at the ducks, nor at the gods. But when Merle read out the poems, she sat up. At the last line she raised her hands and clapped, slowly, twice, the way one claps in a concert hall where one is the only listener. Behind her, in the half-darkness of the room, a man stood holding the curtain aside.`,
    `Merle rang the bell the next morning. His name was Gotthilf Mayer, he had been a master carpenter, he was eighty-eight years old, and he had a pencil stub behind his ear as if he had never retired. His wife, he said, hadn't recognised him for two years. She no longer recognised anyone, and as far as he could tell she lived in a time that had never existed. But she had been a German teacher, forty years at the Uhland grammar school, and Hölderlin she still recognised. "I'm not a poet," he said. "I built staircases. But a poem is just a staircase too, where every step has to be the same height. And she prefers hearing it from you rather than from me. I read too slowly, and I'm, you understand, only the carpenter."`,
    `Merle read all through August and half of September. She struck the anecdotes from her programme one by one, the ducks, the cherry leaf, the handshake with the river, and surprisingly the tips didn't drop. On the last day of the season, in October, when the window below the tower had been shut for a week, there was one last slip under the seat. This time it wasn't a poem, and this time the date was right. "She passed away on Sunday, in the armchair by the window. I thank you for every step." And underneath, for the first time, in the same careful hand: "Gotthilf Mayer".`,
  ],
  "de-ein-fingerhut-ruedesheimer": [
    `In the Bremen Ratskeller, behind a grille and under a vaulted ceiling that Napoleon's officers once tried in vain to impress, lies the oldest cask wine in Germany: a Rüdesheimer from the year 1653, a wine that only narrowly missed the Thirty Years' War and has effortlessly outlasted all the later ones. It is almost never drunk. It is less a drink than a monument in liquid form, and anyone who catches sight of it involuntarily lowers their voice, as if what lay in the cask were not wine but a mayor.`,
    `Jan-Hendrik Oltmanns had been cellar master for eleven years, a lean man from Vegesack with the composure of a harbour pilot and the thoroughness of an accountant. Once a year, in mid-September, he measured the level of the Rose, as the cask was called in the house, and entered it in ink in a book that was older than he was. An old wine evaporates. That was known and allowed for. What was not known and, in his view, could not be allowed for was the fact that in each of the last three years the Rose had lost a tiny bit more than physics required. Four millilitres. Barely more than a thimbleful.`,
    `Someone else would have blamed the measuring device. Oltmanns blamed a person, not out of mistrust but out of respect for the measuring device. He went through the list of those who had access to the vault: two cooks who preferred beer, a trainee who drank nothing at all, a night watchman who had been afraid of the cellar ever since a guest had jokingly told him that the twelve Apostle casks in the next vault talked to each other at night. And then there was the key that had been missing for years and that nobody missed, because the lock was going to be replaced anyway, and had been for years.`,
    `It was the night watchman who put him on the right track without meaning to. The guest who had told him the story of the talking casks, he said, had cited a book "by some poet, long dead". Oltmanns didn't have to search for long. In 1827 Wilhelm Hauff had published his "Fantasies in the Bremen Ratskeller", the story of a night that a young man spends alone in the vault until the wines turn into figures and the Rose appears as a dignified old lady. The night in which all this happens is the night of the first to the second of September. The measurement took place every year in mid-September.`,
    `On the evening of the first of September Oltmanns scattered fine chalk dust on the flagstones in front of the grille, sat down with a thermos of tea in a niche between the Apostle casks and felt more ridiculous than he had since his confirmation. Towards midnight he heard the lock. Then footsteps, small and even, and the tapping of a walking stick. In the beam of his torch stood a very old lady in a dark blue coat, a silver thimble between thumb and forefinger, and she looked at him without being in the least startled.`,
    `"You're the new one," she said, which after eleven years was a bold claim. Her name was Agathe Wiechers, she was ninety-one years old and the widow of the cellar master before Oltmanns' predecessor. Her husband, she said, had first brought her into this vault in the autumn of 1958 after a seminar on Hauff, against every regulation, and had given her a sip from the Rose out of this thimble. After that, every year, on the same night, for forty-six years. Since his death she had come alone. "I drink for two," she said. "Hence the four millilitres. On my own it would be two. You measure very precisely, young man. He would have liked you."`,
    `Oltmanns was a man of regulations, and in this case the regulation was unambiguous. He thought about it for quite a while, so long that the old lady sat down on a cask, which was also forbidden. Then he took a second vessel out of his bag, the shot glass he had been drinking his tea from, and rinsed it with the last of the tea. "A lady of your age shouldn't have to drink for two," he said. "It's bad for the circulation."`,
    `The wine tasted of sherry, of dust, of walnut and of something for which there was no name, perhaps of time. They drank in silence. When Agathe Wiechers left, she left the key lying on the cask without saying a word about it, and Oltmanns pocketed it, likewise without a word. In September he entered a loss of six millilitres in his book, and in the column for remarks, in his small, upright handwriting: "Evaporation, for poetic reasons."`,
  ],
  "de-die-akademie-der-tiere": [
    `In a forest whose name the records do not reveal, the animals decided one spring that merely living was not enough. They had lived in the forest, said the owl, who had been the first to have this thought and therefore had the right to express it, since time immemorial without ever having settled what a forest actually was. That was unworthy of a thinking creature. So they founded an academy. The owl became president because she wore glasses she didn't need, the badger became secretary because he owned a sett in which minutes could be stored dry, and the stag an honorary member because antlers look good in any group photo.`,
    `The first session was devoted to the rules of procedure, the second to the exceptions to the rules of procedure, the third to the question of whether the blackbird, as a songbird, had a right to vote or merely a right to perform. Only in the fourth session did they get down to business. The fox, who sits in every academy and stands out in none, proposed defining the forest as "the totality of the trees". The owl objected that this did not explain where the forest ended and the clearing began, or whether three spruces by a railway embankment were already a forest or still a difference of opinion. A commission was set up.`,
    `Only the woodpecker's mind was not entirely on the matter. Because of his restless nature he was not a full member but merely a corresponding one and was only allowed to comment in writing, which he did in his own way: he knocked. On a Tuesday in May he knocked particularly hard on the oak in whose hollow the academy met. Outside, he called, men with saws were coming up the logging track. The owl asked him not to knock in out of turn. The badger noted in the minutes: "Unsolicited knocking by the corresponding member. Content not usable." And the fox remarked that anyone who came talking about logging tracks was, as everyone knew, on the wrong track himself.`,
    `The commission worked thoroughly. It distinguished between the forest in itself and the forest for us, between the forest as the wild boars experienced it, namely as a larder, and the forest as the deer experienced it, namely as a hiding place. A young dormouse submitted a dissertation on the forest during hibernation that was generally regarded as groundbreaking, although nobody had read it to the end while awake. The result could be summed up in one sentence, which the badger spread over three pages: the forest was more than the sum of its trees.`,
    `Meanwhile the sum of its trees was getting smaller. You could hear it if you listened, and nobody listened, because the debates were loud. The woodpecker knocked a few more times, then less and less often, since the trees he could have knocked on were growing fewer. In the end he moved away, to a mixed forest on the far side of the motorway, and from there sent a last, very short written statement, which the badger filed unopened, since it had not been written on the prescribed bark paper.`,
    `The final session took place in the autumn, and, since the old oak was by now a pile of firewood at the side of the track, it was held on a tree stump. There was just enough room on it for the president. The other members stood around in the undergrowth, and for the first time since the founding of the academy they all had a clear view of the horizon. The owl opened the session with a word of thanks to the commission and stated that they had made great progress on the matter. The stag, who never said anything, said it had got rather draughty.`,
    `The minutes of this session have survived. They end with the sentence: "The question of the essence of the forest could not be conclusively settled for want of a subject. The academy is adjourned." It was later claimed that the animals saw the forest for the first time when there were no more trees standing in the way. That isn't quite right. They didn't see it. But they missed it, and that, as the badger noted in a footnote, is epistemologically almost the same thing.`,
    `Moral: whoever cannot see the forest for the trees is to be pitied. Whoever cannot see it for the concepts is to be feared, for he founds an academy. And whoever knocks to give a warning should, before he knocks, apply for admission as a full member.`,
  ],
};

